# Changelog

- 0.2.3 (unreleased)
  * `traceOf` and `diffOf` take an `env`, set for every run of the
    harness: a runtime that reads its settings as it starts (Go's
    GOMAXPROCS and GOGC) is given them there. A Go harness's runs of
    one secret, recorded without them, laid memory out differently
    from run to run where no secret did.

- 0.2.2 (2026-11-03)
  * `record` runs a guest that starts threads, recording its own
    thread alone, the one its main function runs on; the others run
    unrecorded. The plugin appends how many threads ran, and `diff`
    reports the most any run started (`threads`).
  * The recorded thread runs alone: the others run only while it
    waits in a system call (a futex wait, a sleep, a yield, a poll),
    and it goes on once each of them waits too, or has ended, so
    that what they leave in memory it shares is the same in every
    run. A thread that never blocks holds it back ten seconds at
    most, noted on standard error.
  * The guest's clocks are each thread's own, advancing a
    microsecond at each reading from a fixed start
    (qemu-clock.patch): a guest deciding by time decides alike in
    every run.
  * A differential trace begins at the recorded thread's first
    secret mark (memcheck's MAKE_MEM_UNDEFINED, which the plugin
    knows by its instructions); what ran before it is set aside. A
    run that never marks one is recorded whole.
  * memcheck's leak summary is passed over unread: valgrind 3.26
    leaves it unclosed for a Go library's run.

- 0.2.1 (2026-11-02)
  * `record` starts each run in a PID namespace of its own, so that
    every run sees one pid: a guest that keeps its pid (a random
    generator noting it at seeding, as Mbed TLS's does) differed from
    run to run where no secret did. QEMU is the namespace's second
    process; its first (vestigia-init) starts it and says how it
    ended, a fatal signal included. Needs util-linux's `unshare` and
    unprivileged user namespaces (VESTIGIA_UNSHARE, VESTIGIA_INIT).

- 0.2.0 (2026-10-31)
  * Data-independent-timing contracts: `record --contract arm-dit`
    or `intel-doitm`, with `--object` and `--objdump`, has the plugin
    fingerprint the operands of every instruction outside the ISA's
    contract in the objects named, as it does a division's. The
    tables are transcribed from the vendors' statements: Arm's A64
    ISA XML (2022-12), every encoding with its source registers, and
    Intel's list 768547 (2026-02-23).
  * `diff` reports such an instruction on other operands under
    `outside`, apart from the origins, with its text, and how many
    ran (vestigia/diff-v4, which also names the ISA and contract, and
    gives an operand origin its text where the watch list named it).
    Runs recorded under other contracts are refused.
  * `vestigia watch` writes the watch list once, for `record --watch`
    to read in every run of one program; `diffOf` does so.
  * `record` appends the guest's ISA to its dump.
  * `diffOf` takes a `contract`; the command finds LLVM's
    `llvm-objdump` through `$VESTIGIA_OBJDUMP`.
  * `read` resolves the objects a harness reports to the files they
    name, as memcheck names a frame's object, so a library reached by
    its soname's link has its sites placed by offset.
  * `record --cpu MODEL` has QEMU emulate MODEL (its -cpu), which the
    dump records (a `P` line); `diff` refuses runs recorded on other
    models and names the model in its report; `diffOf` takes `cpu`.
  * QEMU's AArch64 models take `crypto=off`, removing the
    cryptographic extension as a CPU without it reports
    (qemu-crypto.patch).

- 0.1.0 (2026-10-22)
  * Initial version, with two instruments over a harness that marks
    its secret (`vestigia.h`, defined by `vestigia.c`, which serves
    a foreign function interface too and records where the loader
    put each object). A taint run that marked no secret is refused.
  * `vestigia run`: the harness under memcheck with the secret's
    bytes undefined, reporting each branch, address, and integer
    division operand computed from them (the last through a memcheck
    patch, `nix/memcheck-operand.patch`), each at its own
    instruction. `--dir` keeps a run, and `vestigia read` reports on
    several kept runs, or memcheck XML made elsewhere, as one
    (vestigia/trace-v2: each run with its command, exit, and
    objects; each site with the runs that reached it).
  * `vestigia record` and `vestigia diff`: the harness under QEMU
    with vestigia's plugin, its leakage trace summarised per
    instruction, and where runs differing only in the secret diverge
    (vestigia/diff-v3):
    * each run with how it was made, its arguments and exit status;
    * the origins: a branch taking its outcomes in another proportion
      or order, an instruction touching other addresses, and an
      integer division on other operands, a return never one; each
      with the runs alike there, the runs that never reached it, and
      the calls that reached it;
    * what ran downstream, and what every run executed, per object;
    * controls repeating a secret, what differs in them set apart as
      unsteady, and runs designed to reach particular paths, compared
      for control and coverage alone (`--designed`).
  * `record` runs an x86_64 or aarch64 harness on either host, with
    randomisation off (or not at all), the guest's entropy, its
    getrandom included, seeded, and QEMU's own allocations kept off
    the guest's mappings, which under load can otherwise take the
    room of a guest library or heap. The plugin names each
    instruction by its address in its object, and stops a guest that
    starts a second thread.
  * `diff` refuses runs that loaded an object at different
    addresses; runs of another program, or with arguments or an
    environment of another size, than the first run's; a control
    that does not repeat the first run's arguments; and a stopped
    guest's dump. It names sources with LLVM's addr2line.
  * `traceOf` and `diffOf` run either as a derivation, over a list of
    runs; `nix/lib.nix` holds them for any package set, with the
    version the command reports.
  * `--object` keeps a taint run's sites (`run`, `read`, and
    `traceOf`'s `objects`) to objects whose path contains the given
    text, as it keeps a differential run's origins.
  * The flake's probe check (`checks.<system>.probe`, on Linux) holds
    both instruments' reports on a known harness to what it does, and
    on an x86_64 host the differential one on the same harness built
    for aarch64.
